findings.body:
The PR changes `apps/activitypub/tsconfig.json` from `"strict": true` to `"strict": false`. Nothing in the feature needs this. Every other app in the repo keeps `strict: true`, and this change turns off `strictNullChecks`, `noImplicitAny` and `strictFunctionTypes` for the whole ActivityPub package. Type errors that would have been caught in the new polling and cache code will now pass silently.

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/tsconfig.json" lines="17-21"}

If the flip was needed to get `tsc` green, the real type errors should be fixed instead, and the change should be dropped from this PR.

`handleEnable` sets `loading` to true and then awaits the mutation. The old `try/finally` that reset `loading` was removed, so if `enableBluesky()` rejects (a 4xx, 5xx, a 429, or a network error), `loading` stays true. The effect only resets `loading` when `account.blueskyEnabled` changes, and that never happens on failure. The button stays disabled behind a spinner until the page is reloaded. The rejection also escapes the `onClick` handler as an unhandled promise rejection.

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="48-55"}

The 429 handler in the hook only shows a rate-limit message. It does not clear the loading state.

`confirmHandle` calls `confirmBlueskyHandleMutation.mutateAsync().then(...)` and does not attach a `.catch`. `mutateAsync` rejects on any error, not only 429. The hook's `onError` only handles 429. Any 5xx or network failure during polling therefore becomes an unhandled promise rejection, and it repeats every 5 seconds for up to 60 seconds. The user gets no feedback, and the failures are invisible in the UI.

The 429 path has the opposite problem. `renderRateLimitError()` runs on every tick that gets rate-limited, so the user can see the same rate-limit toast up to 12 times in a row.

::diff{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="68-75"}

The UI gives up after 12 ticks at 5 seconds each, which is about 60 seconds. It then calls disable, which turns off the account's Bluesky sharing. Bridgy Fed account creation can take longer than that. A user who is merely slow gets their sharing rolled back and sees an error. The rollback runs only while the component is mounted, so the copy 'You can leave this page and come back to check the status' is misleading. If the user leaves before the cap, nothing enforces the rollback, and coming back restarts the 60-second window from zero.

The retry counter increments on each tick without waiting for the previous request to finish. If a request takes longer than 5 seconds, requests can overlap.

::code{file="apps/activitypub/src/views/Preferences/components/BlueskySharing.tsx" lines="27-28"}
